      "title": "GSM RP: Dissect Originator Address as a Calling Party BCD Number",
      "message": "GSM RP: Dissect Originator Address as a Calling Party BCD Number\n\n3GPP TS 24.011 8.2.5.1 and 8.2.5.2 mention that octets 3, 4, etc.\nof both the RP-Originator Address and RP-Destination Address are\nthe same as those defined for the Called Party BCD Number IE in\n3GPP TS 24.008. In that spec, 10.5.4.7 and 10.5.4.9 shows that\nCalled Party BCD Number and Calling Party BCD Number have the\nsame structure as well, except that in the Called Party BCD Number\nan extension bit is always 1, whereas in Calling Party the extension\nbit might be zero (indicating an extra octet.)\n\nIndeed, inside packet-gsm_a_dtap, both Called and Calling party are\ndissected the same, calling the same helper function with a different\nhfid. (That is, even though the Called Party never has an extension,\nif the extension bit wrong is zero, the extension octet will be added.)\n\nSo dissect the Originator Address with Calling Number, which will\nhave no change other than the GSM RP Originator Address will have\na different hfid used for its digits than the Destination Address,\nso that they can be filtered separately.\nIt is less confusing to have the Originator Address referred to as\na Calling Address in the tree than a Called Address.\n",
